  "@graph": [
    {
      "@id": "study:bert_pre_training_of_deep_bidirectional_transformers_for_language_understanding_2019",
      "@type": "mls:Study",
      "mention": {
        "start_char_doc": 13,
        "end_char_doc": 93,
        "prefix": "## Page 1 #",
        "suffix": "Jacob Devlin Ming-Wei Chang Kenton",
        "valueSource": "extracted"
      },
      "title": {
        "value": "BERT: Pre-training of Deep Bidirectional Transformers for Language Understanding",
        "start_char_doc": 13,
        "end_char_doc": 93,
        "prefix": "## Page 1 #",
        "suffix": "Jacob Devlin Ming-Wei Chang Kenton",
        "valueSource": "extracted"
      },
      "abstract": {
        "value": "We introduce a new language representation model called **BERT**, which stands for **Bidirectional Encoder Representations from Transformers**. Unlike recent language representation models (Peters et al., 2018a; Radford et al., 2018), BERT is designed to pre-train deep bidirectional representations from unlabeled text by jointly conditioning on both left and right context in all layers. As a result, the pre-trained BERT model can be fine-tuned with just one additional output layer to create state-of-the-art models for a wide range of tasks, such as question answering and language inference, without substantial task-specific architecture modifications.\n\nBERT is conceptually simple and empirically powerful. It obtains new state-of-the-art results on eleven natural language processing tasks, including pushing the GLUE score to 80.5% (7.7% point absolute improvement), MultiNLI accuracy to 86.7% (4.6% absolute improvement), SQuAD v1.1 question answering Test F1 to 93.2 (1.5 point absolute improvement) and SQuAD v2.0 Test F1 to 83.1 (5.1 point absolute improvement).",
        "start_char_doc": 318,
        "end_char_doc": 1394,
        "prefix": "24 May 2019&lt;/watermark&gt; ## Abstract",
        "suffix": "## 1 Introduction Language model",
        "valueSource": "extracted"
      },
      "url": {
        "value": "https://arxiv.org/abs/1810.04805v2",
